### In-Depth Study: Java Map Hierarchy, HashMap vs. TreeMap & Key-Value Operations

#### 1. Architecture of Key-Value Mapping:
A `Map` maps unique keys to values. A key cannot be duplicated, but multiple keys can map to identical values.
- In `HashMap`: Key buckets are computed via `key.hashCode()`. When collisions exceed 8 nodes in a bucket (JDK 8+), the bucket converts from a linked list to a balanced red-black tree, maintaining $O(\log n)$ worst-case performance.
- In `TreeMap`: Every key insertion traverses the balanced red-black tree using `compareTo()`, maintaining strict sorting at all times.

#### 2. Detailed Technical Comparison:
| Comparison Parameter | `HashMap` | `TreeMap` | `Hashtable` (Legacy) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Ordering** | None | Strictly Sorted by Key | None |
| **Time Complexity** | **$O(1)$** | **$O(\log n)$** | $O(1)$ |
| **Thread Safety** | Unsynchronized (fast) | Unsynchronized (fast) | Synchronized (slow) |
| **Null Keys / Values** | 1 null key, multiple null values | No null keys, multiple null values | No null keys, No null values |
| **Interfaces Implemented** | `Map`, `Cloneable`, `Serializable` | `NavigableMap`, `SortedMap`, `Map` | `Map`, `Dictionary` |

#### 3. Complete Demonstrative Java Implementation: Word Frequency Counter:
```java
import java.util.*;

public class MapDemonstration {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] words = { "java", "code", "thread", "java", "stream", "code", "java" };

        // Count frequency of words using HashMap
        Map<String, Integer> freqMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (String w : words) {
            freqMap.put(w, freqMap.getOrDefault(w, 0) + 1);
        }

        System.out.println("--- HashMap (Unordered Frequencies) ---");
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : freqMap.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println("  " + entry.getKey() + " => " + entry.getValue());
        }

        // Convert to TreeMap for Alphabetical Sorting
        Map<String, Integer> sortedMap = new TreeMap<>(freqMap);

        System.out.println("\n--- TreeMap (Alphabetically Sorted Keys) ---");
        sortedMap.forEach((k, v) -> System.out.println("  " + k + " => " + v));
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{HashMap provides optimal constant-time key lookups; TreeMap guarantees sorted key traversal.}$$
